who has a ton of certifications.

755
00:35:50,275 --> 00:35:52,152
You also strike me as someone
who's super sharp...

756
00:35:52,177 --> 00:35:53,477
and picks things up right away.

757
00:35:54,079 --> 00:35:56,347
It was written all over her face.

758
00:35:56,548 --> 00:35:57,952
Every time I opened my mouth,
she looked at me like,

759
00:35:57,976 --> 00:35:58,950
"What is this guy's deal?"

760
00:35:58,951 --> 00:36:00,118
That was the vibe she gave off.

761
00:36:00,786 --> 00:36:02,687
Those two hours flew by
way faster than I expected!

762
00:36:02,688 --> 00:36:03,855
Seriously.

763
00:36:04,290 --> 00:36:06,457
- Just watching was exhausting.
- I know.

764
00:36:07,526 --> 00:36:10,027
I'm honestly a little intimidated
by the debates now.

765
00:36:10,362 --> 00:36:11,963
It got so intense.

766
00:36:11,964 --> 00:36:15,365
Doing it over text chat
instead of speaking out loud...

767
00:36:15,366 --> 00:36:16,292
completely changes the dynamic.

768
00:36:16,316 --> 00:36:19,071
People come across
totally different when typing...

769
00:36:19,072 --> 00:36:20,805
than they do in person.

770
00:36:21,874 --> 00:36:22,907
Day and night difference.

771
00:36:23,208 --> 00:36:24,842
Plus, there's the anonymity factor.

772
00:36:24,843 --> 00:36:27,211
Some people seemed to put...

773
00:36:27,212 --> 00:36:29,480
their views out there
with zero hesitation,

774
00:36:29,481 --> 00:36:31,348
like, "This is where I stand,
deal with it."

775
00:36:31,349 --> 00:36:32,917
Yet when we first met face to face,

776
00:36:33,219 --> 00:36:35,253
everyone acted so moderate.

777
00:36:35,654 --> 00:36:36,855
"Oh, I'm pretty neutral."

778
00:36:37,796 --> 00:36:39,997
- Yop.
- All right.

779
00:36:40,507 --> 00:36:41,069
Let's go in.

780
00:36:41,094 --> 00:36:42,092
- Thank you.
- No problem.

781
00:36:45,797 --> 00:36:46,930
This is so...

782
00:36:47,833 --> 00:36:50,735
- karen3000.
- I know, tonight's MVP.

783
00:36:51,303 --> 00:36:52,536
I'm dying of curiosity.

784
00:36:52,537 --> 00:36:55,705
It's impossible to guess who that was.

785
00:36:55,706 --> 00:36:57,741
Tell me about it! Zero clue!

786
00:36:59,511 --> 00:37:02,179
How can I drive a wedge between them?

787
00:37:04,082 --> 00:37:05,183
Are you karen3000?

788
00:37:05,584 --> 00:37:06,450
What?

789
00:37:06,785 --> 00:37:08,420
Right, karen3000 could easily be a guy.

790
00:37:08,487 --> 00:37:09,854
Did you think it was a woman?

791
00:37:09,855 --> 00:37:11,388
Yeah, at first.

792
00:37:11,490 --> 00:37:12,589
I assumed it was a woman too.

793
00:37:12,590 --> 00:37:13,891
- Same here.
- Really?

794
00:37:14,359 --> 00:37:17,895
Just from the handle.
Maybe I was being naive.

795
00:37:18,397 --> 00:37:20,531
I just saw the name...

796
00:37:20,532 --> 00:37:22,599
and assumed woman.

797
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:24,035
At that moment,

798
00:37:24,036 --> 00:37:26,871
I figured it had to be one of the girls.

799
00:37:27,339 --> 00:37:28,774
Is Karen strictly a woman's name?

800
00:37:29,408 --> 00:37:31,676
I actually thought karen3000 was a guy.

801
00:37:31,677 --> 00:37:32,643
Me too.

802
00:37:32,644 --> 00:37:33,878
- Really?
- Yeah.

803
00:37:33,879 --> 00:37:35,246
karen3000 could totally be a guy.

804
00:37:35,247 --> 00:37:37,068
Maybe we all jumped to conclusions
because of the name.

805
00:37:39,751 --> 00:37:41,852
Maybe I need to rethink this.

806
00:37:45,757 --> 00:37:47,224
Man, this is tough.

807
00:37:48,894 --> 00:37:50,995
- It really cooled down tonight.
- Did you grab a smoke?

808
00:37:51,129 --> 00:37:52,395
I think we can head out for one now.

809
00:37:52,396 --> 00:37:53,997
I already had one.

810
00:37:54,733 --> 00:37:55,734
How was it?

811
00:37:56,801 --> 00:38:00,738
From the jump,
karen3000 was way too emotional.

812
00:38:01,206 --> 00:38:03,208
And since everyone started
dividing things along gender lines...

813
00:38:03,209 --> 00:38:05,176
Right, I feel like we boxed ourselves
into making it a gender fight.

814
00:38:05,177 --> 00:38:06,277
That made me really uncomfortable.

815
00:38:06,278 --> 00:38:08,312
I just figured, predictably enough,

816
00:38:08,547 --> 00:38:11,249
a topic like this would split the room
straight down gender lines.

817
00:38:11,583 --> 00:38:15,686
I had a feeling it'd turn
into a battle of the sexes.

818
00:38:16,655 --> 00:38:20,024
And with such an even split...

819
00:38:20,025 --> 00:38:22,859
between guys and girls here,

820
00:38:22,860 --> 00:38:25,996
it felt like the exact kind of topic...

821
00:38:25,997 --> 00:38:28,299
designed to push everyone's buttons.

822
00:38:28,300 --> 00:38:29,767
You're probably right.

823
00:38:29,935 --> 00:38:32,237
Initially, I thought the two
who voted yes earlier...

824
00:38:32,471 --> 00:38:34,372
would automatically jump
to the Against side.

825
00:38:34,373 --> 00:38:37,642
And I assumed both were women.

826
00:38:39,111 --> 00:38:42,413
What really bothers me is
that you can't identify things...

827
00:38:42,681 --> 00:38:44,548
like small versus big government,
or left versus right,

828
00:38:44,549 --> 00:38:46,984
just by looking at someone's face.

829
00:38:47,185 --> 00:38:50,821
Gender, on the other hand, is visible
the second you look at someone.

830
00:38:51,157 --> 00:38:54,024
Right now, that's the biggest hurdle
I'm running into.

831
00:38:54,194 --> 00:38:56,827
(Polar Bear)

832
00:38:58,130 --> 00:38:59,063
(Sugar)

833
00:38:59,064 --> 00:39:02,600
Even if our political views
are completely opposite,

834
00:39:02,601 --> 00:39:05,370
do you think we could
still team up, Michael?

835
00:39:05,371 --> 00:39:07,639
Based on what I saw today? In a heartbeat.

836
00:39:08,006 --> 00:39:10,417
Then starting tomorrow, let's start
pulling people into our circle,

837
00:39:10,442 --> 00:39:11,275
step by step.

838
00:39:11,276 --> 00:39:12,944
Sounds like a plan.

839
00:39:14,747 --> 00:39:17,615
The favorability vote has concluded.

840
00:39:18,650 --> 00:39:21,652
All official activities for today
are wrapped up,

841
00:39:21,787 --> 00:39:24,722
and the Community Center is now closed.

842
00:39:24,990 --> 00:39:26,324
Who's down for a beer?

843
00:39:26,325 --> 00:39:27,658
Count me in!

844
00:39:28,260 --> 00:39:29,560
Quick question for you guys.

845
00:39:29,561 --> 00:39:31,929
Did any of you know each other
before coming on the show?

846
00:39:32,097 --> 00:39:33,632
Were there any mutuals beforehand?

847
00:39:35,334 --> 00:39:36,734
I don't think so.

848
00:39:44,976 --> 00:39:46,810
So everyone's just a phenomenal actor.

849
00:39:47,614 --> 00:39:49,548
Where'd that come from?

850
00:39:50,449 --> 00:39:51,782
Fascinating.

851
00:39:53,185 --> 00:39:57,188
One thing today's debate
made crystal clear...

852
00:39:57,189 --> 00:40:01,893
is that political alignment
plays a massive role here.

853
00:40:02,060 --> 00:40:04,261
At the end of the day, we're our own jury.

854
00:40:04,930 --> 00:40:07,764
When I first heard the rules,
it felt bizarre.

855
00:40:08,299 --> 00:40:11,435
It's a debate,
yet there are no outside, neutral judges.

856
00:40:11,436 --> 00:40:14,872
We have to evaluate each other.
I doubted it would work.

857
00:40:15,073 --> 00:40:18,308
Seeing the results tonight, though,

858
00:40:18,477 --> 00:40:20,644
it clicked that having us vote
is the whole point.

859
00:40:20,645 --> 00:40:22,547
The final score reflected
the political dynamics in the room.

860
00:40:22,548 --> 00:40:24,582
Exactly. It was purely political.

861
00:40:24,583 --> 00:40:25,383
Totally.

862
00:40:25,817 --> 00:40:28,185
I don't know who karen3000 is,

863
00:40:28,420 --> 00:40:31,156
but looking at how the room voted,
it makes sense they bagged MVP.

864
00:40:31,724 --> 00:40:34,391
Under these rules, at least.

865
00:40:35,827 --> 00:40:37,529
She does know how to type.

866
00:40:37,696 --> 00:40:39,130
Yeah, she does know how to type.

867
00:40:39,331 --> 00:40:41,598
- And she does it very fast.
- Very fast.

868
00:40:41,599 --> 00:40:42,400
Yes.

869
00:40:42,667 --> 00:40:44,636
You two should probably take this
somewhere private.

870
00:40:45,210 --> 00:40:47,644
My bad.

871
00:40:49,040 --> 00:40:50,842
Funny that you referred
to karen3000 as "she."

872
00:40:51,276 --> 00:40:53,010
We were actually just debating that,

873
00:40:53,011 --> 00:40:55,746
and everyone had different hunches
about karen3000's gender.

874
00:40:56,515 --> 00:40:58,783
You really can't tell
from a screen name alone.

875
00:40:58,784 --> 00:41:00,051
True.

876
00:41:00,052 --> 00:41:01,419
It's a total guessing game.

877
00:41:01,420 --> 00:41:04,388
I'm pretty sure the term "Karen" has...

878
00:41:04,528 --> 00:41:07,691
a super negative connotation
in the States.

879
00:41:07,692 --> 00:41:08,960
- Really?
- You knew that?

880
00:41:09,427 --> 00:41:10,695
A negative connotation?

881
00:41:10,696 --> 00:41:12,629
It's basically a meme.

882
00:41:12,898 --> 00:41:16,567
In the US, people use it to describe
entitled white women...

883
00:41:16,902 --> 00:41:20,539
who throw tantrums at retail workers...

884
00:41:20,540 --> 00:41:22,606
- or act completely out of line.
- Seriously?

885
00:41:22,607 --> 00:41:23,806
So it's an insult.

886
00:41:23,807 --> 00:41:25,176
A derogatory label
for that type of white women.

887
00:41:25,177 --> 00:41:28,412
Which made me think karen3000 might...

888
00:41:28,947 --> 00:41:30,447
actually be a guy.

889
00:41:30,982 --> 00:41:32,349
Actually be a guy.

890
00:41:32,785 --> 00:41:34,385
Could be. Absolutely.

891
00:41:35,353 --> 00:41:37,321
Knowing what that slang means...

892
00:41:37,556 --> 00:41:40,324
You wouldn't pick karen3000
unless you knew the joke behind it.

893
00:41:40,325 --> 00:41:41,458
Exactly.

894
00:41:41,459 --> 00:41:44,795
Even the way they behaved
during the debate,

895
00:41:45,464 --> 00:41:46,830
She acted like a Karen.

896
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:52,603
In a way,
they might just be leaning into...

897
00:41:52,806 --> 00:41:54,872
their assigned role really well.

898
00:41:55,674 --> 00:41:56,907
So they could be the spy.

899
00:41:57,109 --> 00:41:59,710
- I wouldn't put it past them.
- High probability.

900
00:42:00,011 --> 00:42:01,912
And they scooped up the MVP on top of it.

901
00:42:02,047 --> 00:42:04,281
- Spot on.
- The spy just walked away with MVP.

902
00:42:04,548 --> 00:42:07,217
Trolling their way right to MVP.

903
00:42:07,218 --> 00:42:09,654
Sometimes the most obvious spot
is the easiest to overlook.

904
00:42:11,556 --> 00:42:12,924
So who's the Karen?

905
00:42:15,260 --> 00:42:16,193
Who could it be?

906
00:42:17,929 --> 00:42:19,964
It's like being handed an easy question,

907
00:42:19,965 --> 00:42:21,765
nailing the answer,

908
00:42:22,066 --> 00:42:24,268
and then overthinking what the catch is
until you blow it.

909
00:42:24,269 --> 00:42:25,770
We're not analyzing high literature here.

910
00:42:25,771 --> 00:42:27,972
The risk was obvious from day one.

911
00:42:28,139 --> 00:42:31,208
It could be someone like Michael,
someone ethnically Korean...

912
00:42:31,209 --> 00:42:32,977
who grew up overseas.

913
00:42:33,111 --> 00:42:34,921
Those were all variables
I should've seen coming.

914
00:42:34,946 --> 00:42:37,581
You know how some serial killers...

915
00:42:37,582 --> 00:42:39,986
get caught because their ego
won't let them stay away,

916
00:42:39,987 --> 00:42:42,419
so they return to the crime scene
just to admire their work?

917
00:42:42,420 --> 00:42:44,621
That exact kind of arrogance
is what sinks you.

918
00:42:44,890 --> 00:42:46,490
I made a boneheaded mistake.

919
00:42:46,491 --> 00:42:48,092
Nothing to do but own it.

920
00:42:48,093 --> 00:42:50,194
I'll keep fighting, obviously, but...

921
00:42:52,564 --> 00:42:53,765
Damn it. Are you kidding me?

922
00:43:02,774 --> 00:43:06,010
(9 a.m.)

923
00:43:06,511 --> 00:43:07,533
(Dark Knight:
Former special forces, Right)

924
00:43:07,557 --> 00:43:08,612
(Equalism, Working-class, Traditionalist)

925
00:43:09,014 --> 00:43:11,849
(Michael: Rapper, Right, Equalism,
Upper-middle, Traditionalist)

926
00:43:12,464 --> 00:43:14,619
(Nangja:
Former bodyguard turned actor, Right)

927
00:43:14,643 --> 00:43:16,220
(Equalism, Working-class, Traditionalist)

928
00:43:16,821 --> 00:43:19,022
(Hippo: Writer, Left, Feminism,
Working-class, Open-minded)

929
00:43:19,023 --> 00:43:20,424
I barely slept last night.

930
00:43:21,426 --> 00:43:23,761
I lay there with my eyes shut,

931
00:43:24,429 --> 00:43:27,297
but my brain was running the entire night.

932
00:43:27,298 --> 00:43:32,403
We're in this game where we're labeled
by these four binary axes,

933
00:43:32,404 --> 00:43:36,607
yet I've realized none of us
can ever truly...

934
00:43:36,608 --> 00:43:38,876
be reduced to a cookie-cutter stereotype.

935
00:43:38,877 --> 00:43:40,777
We're all unique individuals,

936
00:43:40,778 --> 00:43:41,845
and that includes me.

937
00:43:41,846 --> 00:43:43,714
I thought, "I can't let myself..."

938
00:43:44,349 --> 00:43:46,050
"get swept up in all this."

939
00:43:46,051 --> 00:43:49,086
"I need to accept that."

940
00:43:49,087 --> 00:43:51,655
I also realized that I need to open up...

941
00:43:51,656 --> 00:43:54,025
and show people more of who I am first.

942
00:43:54,026 --> 00:43:55,860
Only then will they feel...

943
00:43:55,861 --> 00:43:59,096
more comfortable with me and be able...

944
00:43:59,097 --> 00:44:01,999
to build a relationship with me.

945
00:44:02,133 --> 00:44:05,802
Once I sorted that out in my head,
I felt a little more at ease.

946
00:44:05,803 --> 00:44:07,170
Should we make stir-fried potatoes?

947
00:44:07,305 --> 00:44:08,839
Sounds great! Stir-fried potatoes?

948
00:44:12,911 --> 00:44:14,245
The smell of scallion oil.

949
00:44:16,715 --> 00:44:17,715
Smells amazing.

950
00:44:24,055 --> 00:44:26,723
(She brings out the pu-erh tea
and tea set that she brought with her.)

951
00:44:28,226 --> 00:44:30,527
Look at her. She even brought a teapot.

952
00:44:36,968 --> 00:44:39,902
I think the moment I walked downstairs...

953
00:44:39,903 --> 00:44:42,172
carrying the tea set was
pretty important to me.

954
00:44:42,407 --> 00:44:46,877
I decided that I needed to open up first.

955
00:44:48,079 --> 00:44:49,613
This is for Superman.

956
00:44:50,782 --> 00:44:51,715
Precious tea for me?

957
00:44:52,717 --> 00:44:54,885
I basically...

958
00:44:55,620 --> 00:44:57,220
drink it twice a day.

959
00:44:58,824 --> 00:45:00,491
- Smells nice.
- This is great.

960
00:45:00,492 --> 00:45:02,726
- Right?
- It refreshes you.

961
00:45:04,062 --> 00:45:05,295
It's good for your skin too.

962
00:45:05,964 --> 00:45:07,731
Drinking tea in the morning is amazing.

963
00:45:07,732 --> 00:45:09,867
Right? It's even better
on an empty stomach.

964
00:45:09,868 --> 00:45:10,901
Oh, really?

965
00:45:13,171 --> 00:45:14,404
Good morning.

966
00:45:15,173 --> 00:45:16,640
- Feel free to have this.
- What's that?

967
00:45:16,641 --> 00:45:17,541
Have some tea.

968
00:45:17,675 --> 00:45:18,942
It's a vitamin drink.

969
00:45:20,012 --> 00:45:22,813
Tea is really popular right now.
Everyone's into it.

970
00:45:22,948 --> 00:45:24,548
I normally prefer something like that.

971
00:45:25,016 --> 00:45:26,149
But the tea has my full attention.

972
00:45:31,856 --> 00:45:33,290
He's groaning.

973
00:45:33,992 --> 00:45:34,959
It really is good.

974
00:45:36,770 --> 00:45:38,929
I'm so happy that Superman loves it.

975
00:45:38,930 --> 00:45:39,663
- Right?
- Gosh.

976
00:45:39,664 --> 00:45:41,031
(Groaning)

977
00:45:42,133 --> 00:45:43,401
What are you going to do
with everyone's reactions?

978
00:45:43,402 --> 00:45:45,235
I guess it's good for gym rats.

979
00:45:45,904 --> 00:45:47,371
She said it's from Yunnan.

980
00:45:48,472 --> 00:45:50,874
- From where?
- Yunnan, China.

981
00:45:50,875 --> 00:45:51,775
Obscene?

982
00:45:53,245 --> 00:45:55,747
- Hold on.
- Wake up!

983
00:45:55,748 --> 00:45:57,314
That's a dad joke.

984
00:45:57,849 --> 00:45:58,849
He loves it, doesn't he?

985
00:45:59,885 --> 00:46:01,586
(All residents are now awake.)

986
00:46:01,587 --> 00:46:03,720
I can't believe someone was still asleep!

987
00:46:03,721 --> 00:46:06,189
Six iced Americanos, please.

988
00:46:06,190 --> 00:46:08,592
And one hot Americano too.

989
00:46:08,593 --> 00:46:09,993
The purchase has been completed.

990
00:46:10,295 --> 00:46:11,628
It will be deducted
from the communal fund.

991
00:46:12,030 --> 00:46:14,531
We can spend about 1,600 dollars
on breakfast, right?

992
00:46:15,567 --> 00:46:16,567
Sure.

993
00:46:16,867 --> 00:46:18,502
We went through so much yesterday.

994
00:46:18,503 --> 00:46:21,472
- I have a question.
- Yes?

995
00:46:21,473 --> 00:46:23,774
I saw a post on the board
that I could really relate to.

996
00:46:24,609 --> 00:46:27,244
(Social Media, Oikos:
Residents can chat anonymously...)

997
00:46:27,245 --> 00:46:29,380
(and post on the feed through Oikos.)

998
00:46:29,381 --> 00:46:31,549
(Anonymous: I hope
I'm just worrying for nothing,)

999
00:46:31,550 --> 00:46:33,784
(but after watching the debate, I'm afraid
that people may end up voting...)

1000
00:46:33,785 --> 00:46:35,886
(based on what they already believe
rather than on which side did better...)

1001
00:46:35,887 --> 00:46:37,888
(or who led the debate more effectively.
They may simply give...)

1002
00:46:37,889 --> 00:46:40,356
(the MVP vote to whoever best represented
the position they already agreed with.)

1003
00:46:40,357 --> 00:46:42,660
It was basically questioning
whether people...

1004
00:46:42,961 --> 00:46:45,128
with fixed opinions
from the start simply voted...

1005
00:46:45,129 --> 00:46:47,897
according to those beliefs,
regardless of how the debate went.

1006
00:46:47,898 --> 00:46:50,400
(Post your reply)

1007
00:46:50,401 --> 00:46:52,269
(Anonymous:
Yes, you're worrying for nothing.)

1008
00:46:52,270 --> 00:46:53,803
I read it too.

1009
00:46:54,372 --> 00:46:56,774
- Me too.
- I was curious about that too.

1010
00:46:56,775 --> 00:46:58,975
Since yesterday's debate didn't
go very smoothly,

1011
00:46:58,976 --> 00:47:01,312
I wondered whether your initial opinions
simply carried through to the final vote.

1012
00:47:01,313 --> 00:47:04,815
I think it would be good to talk
a little about yesterday's debate.

1013
00:47:04,816 --> 00:47:05,849
Sounds good.

1014
00:47:05,850 --> 00:47:09,752
What did you think of yesterday's debate?

1015
00:47:09,753 --> 00:47:12,189
I think it would be better
to have a moderator.

1016
00:47:12,489 --> 00:47:14,357
I don't know if this would be possible,

1017
00:47:14,358 --> 00:47:16,292
but one of the six people
who aren't debating...

1018
00:47:16,694 --> 00:47:18,128
could act as the moderator.

1019
00:47:18,129 --> 00:47:20,964
The other five could leave comments
suggesting what they'd like...

1020
00:47:20,965 --> 00:47:22,132
the debaters to discuss further.

1021
00:47:22,799 --> 00:47:24,501
The moderator could reflect
those suggestions...

1022
00:47:24,502 --> 00:47:26,803
and guide the debate
when necessary by saying,

1023
00:47:27,138 --> 00:47:29,206
"Let's focus on this issue for now."

1024
00:47:30,008 --> 00:47:33,711
I actually think
it would be better to agree...

1025
00:47:33,878 --> 00:47:37,681
on clear judging criteria
rather than appointing a single moderator.

1026
00:47:37,682 --> 00:47:39,283
But that could actually end up...

1027
00:47:39,284 --> 00:47:41,852
reinforcing people's biases.

1028
00:47:41,853 --> 00:47:44,654
With debates, it can be hard to set
clear judging criteria in the first place.

1029
00:47:44,655 --> 00:47:47,123
I think the key is
to have a cooling-off period.

1030
00:47:47,124 --> 00:47:50,126
If we give the moderator
too much authority,

1031
00:47:50,127 --> 00:47:51,595
I think that could undermine...

1032
00:47:51,596 --> 00:47:54,364
the whole point of having
everyone participate in the chat.

1033
00:47:54,365 --> 00:47:55,799
Still, I don't think...

1034
00:47:55,800 --> 00:47:57,701
the discussion should go
too far off track.

1035
00:47:57,702 --> 00:47:59,736
So during the cooling-off period,

1036
00:47:59,737 --> 00:48:02,939
if several people post on the board
that the discussion is spiraling,

1037
00:48:02,940 --> 00:48:05,976
the debaters can check the feed,

1038
00:48:05,977 --> 00:48:07,511
talk it over in the chat,

1039
00:48:07,512 --> 00:48:08,712
and then resume the debate.

1040
00:48:08,713 --> 00:48:10,146
It's good to hear different opinions,

1041
00:48:10,147 --> 00:48:11,749
but everyone's opinion is going
to be different.

1042
00:48:11,750 --> 00:48:15,185
In the end, it's still up
to the six debaters to decide...

1043
00:48:15,186 --> 00:48:17,221
- which suggestions to take on board.
- Right.

1044
00:48:17,222 --> 00:48:19,156
And without a clear direction,

1045
00:48:19,157 --> 00:48:20,723
the discussion could
just go off track again.

1046
00:48:20,724 --> 00:48:23,894
But the moderator will eventually
be voting too, right?

1047
00:48:23,895 --> 00:48:26,296
They're not truly neutral to begin with,

1048
00:48:26,297 --> 00:48:28,032
so I'm a little concerned...

1049
00:48:28,033 --> 00:48:30,433
that giving them too much authority...

1050
00:48:30,434 --> 00:48:32,869
might actually make the debate...

1051
00:48:32,870 --> 00:48:33,937
less fair.

1052
00:48:33,938 --> 00:48:36,239
- That's why I...
- The moderator would...

1053
00:48:36,240 --> 00:48:38,642
probably feel uncomfortable getting
too involved anyway,

1054
00:48:38,643 --> 00:48:40,511
since that could reveal
something about them.

1055
00:48:40,512 --> 00:48:43,713
So I don't think they'd be able
to intervene that much in the first place.

1056
00:48:43,714 --> 00:48:45,916
So in the end,
having a moderator could create...

1057
00:48:45,917 --> 00:48:48,518
even more debate
over the moderator's role,

1058
00:48:48,519 --> 00:48:50,253
which makes assigning
that role even more complicated.

1059
00:48:50,254 --> 00:48:52,155
I don't think we should lump
those issues together...

1060
00:48:52,156 --> 00:48:55,392
- because then, we'd have to start...
- But if we don't have that discussion,

1061
00:48:55,393 --> 00:48:58,528
then we'd have a moderator
with no real role.

1062
00:48:58,529 --> 00:49:01,197
I still think someone needs to gather
and reflect the different opinions...

1063
00:49:01,198 --> 00:49:02,299
of the six debaters.

1064
00:49:02,300 --> 00:49:04,935
But that assumes the six debaters are...

1065
00:49:04,936 --> 00:49:06,903
incapable of doing that themselves.

1066
00:49:06,904 --> 00:49:09,741
I think we're all capable
of thinking things...

1067
00:49:09,742 --> 00:49:11,006
through to that extent.

1068
00:49:11,007 --> 00:49:11,975
Now that you've heard everyone's opinions,

1069
00:49:11,976 --> 00:49:14,544
please raise your hand if you think
we should have a moderator.

1070
00:49:15,012 --> 00:49:16,179
(We should have a moderator.)

1071
00:49:16,180 --> 00:49:17,213
Two.

1072
00:49:17,214 --> 00:49:19,249
(Superman, Genie)

1073
00:49:19,250 --> 00:49:21,851
Please raise your hand if you think
we shouldn't have a moderator.

1074
00:49:22,054 --> 00:49:23,353
Who thinks we shouldn't have a moderator?

1075
00:49:23,354 --> 00:49:26,590
(No moderator:
Everyone except Superman and Genie)

1076
00:49:26,824 --> 00:49:27,924
- Got it.
- I see.

1077
00:49:27,925 --> 00:49:30,160
By majority vote, we've decided
not to have a moderator,

1078
00:49:30,161 --> 00:49:31,862
- but we will have a cooling-off period.
- Okay.

1079
00:49:31,863 --> 00:49:33,663
I still want to raise one concern.

1080
00:49:33,664 --> 00:49:37,334
I think someone needs
to provide some guidance...

1081
00:49:37,335 --> 00:49:40,237
so that we can make
the most of this two-hour debate.

1082
00:49:40,640 --> 00:49:41,672
That's what I think.

1083
00:49:42,206 --> 00:49:43,807
The vote is over for now, so...

1084
00:49:43,808 --> 00:49:46,276
Let's keep talking about it.

1085
00:49:47,779 --> 00:49:50,814
I did add to Superman's point,

1086
00:49:50,815 --> 00:49:53,851
but even after the majority had made
its decision,

1087
00:49:53,852 --> 00:49:56,653
he kept expressing his opinion.

1088
00:49:56,654 --> 00:49:59,389
On and on.

1089
00:49:59,390 --> 00:50:02,760
That made me realize
he has very strong views.

1090
00:50:02,761 --> 00:50:05,462
It does make me curious to see...

1091
00:50:05,463 --> 00:50:08,166
what he'd be like
if he took on a leadership role.

1092
00:50:08,167 --> 00:50:10,434
Superman and Polar Bear had...

1093
00:50:10,435 --> 00:50:12,469
a major clash of opinions.

1094
00:50:12,470 --> 00:50:16,039
Anyone could see that their views
and personalities were very different.

1095
00:50:16,040 --> 00:50:18,408
It can be messy if you put
two strong people against each other.

1096
00:50:18,409 --> 00:50:20,311
They're both stubborn. Neither of them
really listens to the other.

1097
00:50:20,312 --> 00:50:22,713
As someone who's here to sow chaos,
I found it very entertaining.

1098
00:50:22,714 --> 00:50:24,348
But these people are way too civilized.

1099
00:50:24,349 --> 00:50:28,019
How do you end up making rules
for an anonymous debate?

1100
00:50:28,020 --> 00:50:29,253
I guess they just couldn't tolerate it.

1101
00:50:29,254 --> 00:50:31,054
They couldn't stand...

1102
00:50:31,055 --> 00:50:33,824
someone like Karen coming in and making...

1103
00:50:33,825 --> 00:50:34,858
a mess of the chat.

1104
00:50:34,859 --> 00:50:35,959
They're very civilized people.

1105
00:50:36,427 --> 00:50:39,563
I really like how we're building
things one by one like this.

1106
00:50:39,564 --> 00:50:41,898
We're literally creating
the rules of our own community.

1107
00:50:41,899 --> 00:50:44,068
It almost feels like we're building
a country.

1108
00:50:44,362 --> 00:50:46,162
Like a government is starting
to take shape.

1109
00:50:46,437 --> 00:50:48,205
But it doesn't seem easy.

1110
00:50:48,206 --> 00:50:50,774
In a way, this is what it means
for a community to form.

1111
00:50:50,775 --> 00:50:52,610
- Right. We're making rules.
- We're creating order...

1112
00:50:52,611 --> 00:50:53,977
little by little from chaos.

1113
00:50:56,948 --> 00:50:59,783
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

1114
00:51:00,685 --> 00:51:03,153
There is an important new announcement.

1115
00:51:05,490 --> 00:51:09,827
Starting today, residents may conduct
"ideology checks" on one another.

1116
00:51:12,331 --> 00:51:13,230
"Ideology?"

1117
00:51:13,365 --> 00:51:14,497
Starting today?

1118
00:51:15,666 --> 00:51:20,303
Now, let me explain the rules
for the ideology check.

1119
00:51:21,205 --> 00:51:25,375
Before entering the Community House,

1120
00:51:25,376 --> 00:51:28,044
each resident was assigned a score...

1121
00:51:28,713 --> 00:51:31,348
in four categories
based on a preliminary test...

1122
00:51:31,349 --> 00:51:34,217
on politics, gender, class, and openness.

1123
00:51:35,487 --> 00:51:39,322
Here are the total code scores
for all residents.

1124
00:51:40,992 --> 00:51:43,293
(Code Scores)

1125
00:51:43,294 --> 00:51:46,930
From this moment on,
all residents' total code scores...

1126
00:51:47,498 --> 00:51:51,100
can also be viewed
on the monitors in the bedrooms.

1127
00:51:52,069 --> 00:51:55,037
However, the detailed score
for each individual code...

1128
00:51:55,038 --> 00:51:56,906
will only be revealed
to the resident themselves.

1129
00:51:57,575 --> 00:51:59,009
(Gray: Eight)

1130
00:51:59,010 --> 00:52:00,811
(Superman: Seven)

1131
00:52:00,812 --> 00:52:02,712
(Nangja: Five)

1132
00:52:02,713 --> 00:52:05,482
(Michael: 5, Polar Bear: 10, Ted: 7)

1133
00:52:05,483 --> 00:52:07,317
(Dark Knight: Eight)

1134
00:52:07,318 --> 00:52:09,152
(Hippo: 11)

1135
00:52:09,153 --> 00:52:10,322
We're the lowest.

1136
00:52:10,323 --> 00:52:13,657
The ideology check will proceed
as follows.

1137
00:52:14,559 --> 00:52:18,661
First, starting today,
you may visit the Community Center...

1138
00:52:18,863 --> 00:52:22,765
and say, "I'd like to conduct
an ideology check,"

1139
00:52:23,434 --> 00:52:26,036
to receive an ideology check form.

1140
00:52:27,005 --> 00:52:28,137
Second,

1141
00:52:28,407 --> 00:52:30,508
fill in the name of the resident
you wish to attack,

1142
00:52:30,509 --> 00:52:32,310
along with their exact score
for each code...

1143
00:52:32,311 --> 00:52:35,479
and submit the form.

1144
00:52:36,080 --> 00:52:38,181
The ideology check will
then be carried out.

1145
00:52:38,716 --> 00:52:39,582
Attack?

1146
00:52:40,051 --> 00:52:41,218
Third,

1147
00:52:41,686 --> 00:52:43,554
if the attack is successful,

1148
00:52:44,021 --> 00:52:46,456
the resident who was attacked will be...

1149
00:52:46,457 --> 00:52:47,590
eliminated the following morning,

1150
00:52:47,959 --> 00:52:49,426
and all of their personal funds
will be transferred...

1151
00:52:49,427 --> 00:52:52,195
to the resident
who successfully attacked them.

1152
00:52:56,400 --> 00:52:57,533
- I'm getting chills.
- Fourth,

1153
00:52:58,270 --> 00:53:00,137
if the attack fails,

1154
00:53:00,873 --> 00:53:02,740
a certain amount will be deducted...

1155
00:53:02,741 --> 00:53:04,374
from the attacking
resident's personal funds,

1156
00:53:04,375 --> 00:53:05,709
(Check failed)

1157
00:53:05,710 --> 00:53:09,246
and they will be told
the codes they got correct.

1158
00:53:09,247 --> 00:53:10,614
(Upper-middle class and openness matched.)

1159
00:53:12,083 --> 00:53:13,050
Fifth,

1160
00:53:13,684 --> 00:53:15,718
if a resident is attacked,

1161
00:53:16,120 --> 00:53:17,886
they will be notified...

1162
00:53:17,887 --> 00:53:20,023
through the monitor in their bedroom.

1163
00:53:20,024 --> 00:53:22,459
Whether the attack succeeded
or failed will be revealed,

1164
00:53:22,894 --> 00:53:26,263
but the attacker's identity
will remain confidential.

1165
00:53:27,565 --> 00:53:28,598
Sixth,

1166
00:53:29,033 --> 00:53:32,302
ideology checks may be conducted
every day from 6 p.m.,

1167
00:53:32,303 --> 00:53:34,838
after the day's official schedule
has ended, until midnight.

1168
00:53:35,206 --> 00:53:37,174
Ideology checks will end on Day 6.

1169
00:53:38,709 --> 00:53:41,377
This concludes the announcement
from the Community Center.

1170
00:53:44,482 --> 00:53:46,750
The mission would never let us live
in peace, would it?

1171
00:53:46,751 --> 00:53:47,918
So this is how the game works.

1172
00:53:47,919 --> 00:53:49,619
This is so hard.

1173
00:53:49,953 --> 00:53:52,689
"So this is how they play the game."

1174
00:53:53,391 --> 00:53:56,893
"And you really can be eliminated?"

1175
00:53:56,894 --> 00:53:57,895
"I don't want to get eliminated!"

1176
00:53:57,896 --> 00:54:01,631
"Right. So this is
where they pull something like this."

1177
00:54:02,099 --> 00:54:04,034
I was surprised in so many ways.

1178
00:54:04,035 --> 00:54:06,002
Finally, they've announced rules...

1179
00:54:06,003 --> 00:54:09,539
that really live up
to the title of this show.

1180
00:54:09,540 --> 00:54:11,675
There might be a way
for all of us to survive.

1181
00:54:11,676 --> 00:54:12,942
Or a way for all of us
to go down together.

1182
00:54:12,943 --> 00:54:14,644
You're right.

1183
00:54:15,781 --> 00:54:17,414
So it really is a survival game.

1184
00:54:17,415 --> 00:54:18,282
You're right.

1185
00:54:18,283 --> 00:54:20,083
Does that mean only one person
can be eliminated per day?

1186
00:54:20,851 --> 00:54:23,053
I think multiple people can be
eliminated if the attacks succeed.

1187
00:54:23,054 --> 00:54:24,821
Several people can be
eliminated at once...

1188
00:54:24,822 --> 00:54:26,390
since they didn't say otherwise.

1189
00:54:26,391 --> 00:54:29,926
So we're not just guessing
whether someone is left or right,

1190
00:54:29,927 --> 00:54:32,128
but their exact score in each category?

1191
00:54:32,129 --> 00:54:34,831
Yes, based on the total score.

1192
00:54:34,832 --> 00:54:35,799
That doesn't sound easy.

1193
00:54:35,800 --> 00:54:36,866
It's very difficult.

1194
00:54:36,867 --> 00:54:40,104
Statistically, people with higher scores
are easier to guess...

1195
00:54:40,105 --> 00:54:41,971
because they're closer to the extremes.

1196
00:54:41,972 --> 00:54:42,905
Save me!

1197
00:54:43,341 --> 00:54:45,341
Save me. Don't you want to keep
drinking this tea?

1198
00:54:45,342 --> 00:54:47,544
(Hippo: 11)

1199
00:54:47,545 --> 00:54:49,046
(Polar Bear: Ten)

1200
00:54:49,047 --> 00:54:50,347
It's only Day Two!

1201
00:54:50,348 --> 00:54:52,482
It's too early for us to fall apart.

1202
00:54:52,483 --> 00:54:53,717
It's only Day Two.

1203
00:54:54,518 --> 00:54:56,419
I was honestly shocked.

1204
00:54:56,420 --> 00:54:58,588
I had no idea our scores were going
to be revealed like that.

1205
00:54:58,589 --> 00:55:01,023
And mine is the highest right now.

1206
00:55:01,024 --> 00:55:02,258
I'm in serious trouble.

1207
00:55:02,259 --> 00:55:04,594
I don't think I was able
to hide it on my face at all.

1208
00:55:04,962 --> 00:55:06,795
I kept imagining myself...

1209
00:55:06,796 --> 00:55:09,632
practically begging the people...

1210
00:55:10,401 --> 00:55:12,502
who had been leaders and had immunity.

1211
00:55:13,104 --> 00:55:14,638
I just kept thinking,
"I'm really in trouble."

1212
00:55:14,906 --> 00:55:15,839
This is interesting.

1213
00:55:15,840 --> 00:55:17,641
But how are we supposed to figure out...

1214
00:55:17,642 --> 00:55:19,109
someone's exact score
just from a few conversations?

1215
00:55:19,110 --> 00:55:20,143
Based on the debate?

1216
00:55:20,412 --> 00:55:22,112
- We'll have to see.
- Right.

1217
00:55:22,113 --> 00:55:23,880
I think we're supposed
to piece together several clues.

1218
00:55:24,114 --> 00:55:27,850
They want us to start matching
the anonymous chat to real people.

1219
00:55:27,851 --> 00:55:29,919
"Who do you think said that?"

1220
00:55:30,221 --> 00:55:32,656
And judging from the topic
they gave us last night,

1221
00:55:32,923 --> 00:55:34,391
I think that was also a hint...

1222
00:55:34,392 --> 00:55:36,325
that we should use those discussions
to figure people out.

1223
00:55:37,995 --> 00:55:41,097
I just thought, "I need
to figure out everyone's ideology today."

1224
00:55:41,632 --> 00:55:42,905
It comes down to one of two things.

1225
00:55:42,906 --> 00:55:45,668
Either you target someone
you want to eliminate,

1226
00:55:45,669 --> 00:55:49,005
or you go after someone whose ideology...

1227
00:55:49,006 --> 00:55:51,006
you have a better chance
of guessing correctly.

1228
00:55:51,007 --> 00:55:54,778
Seems like a lot of people are targeting
those with high scores right now,

1229
00:55:54,779 --> 00:55:57,413
but I actually think
people with lower scores are easier.

1230
00:55:57,414 --> 00:56:01,184
If someone has a total score of 4 or 5,
you only need to get about one point.

1231
00:56:01,919 --> 00:56:02,933
If we gang up on someone,

1232
00:56:03,513 --> 00:56:06,055
then they're basically guaranteed
to be eliminated, right?

1233
00:56:06,266 --> 00:56:08,166
- Probably, if enough people target them.
- You can if you want.

1234
00:56:08,191 --> 00:56:09,458
Oh, so that could happen.

1235
00:56:09,459 --> 00:56:10,893
One strategy would be to form an alliance.

1236
00:56:11,262 --> 00:56:13,096
There aren't
that many possible combinations, right?

1237
00:56:13,531 --> 00:56:14,798
So if an alliance agrees to attack...

1238
00:56:14,799 --> 00:56:16,900
the same person all at once,

1239
00:56:16,901 --> 00:56:18,935
as long as one of them gets it right,
that person gets eliminated.

1240
00:56:18,936 --> 00:56:20,370
Even with just three people,
you'd probably be able to figure it out.

1241
00:56:20,371 --> 00:56:22,072
They said they'll tell you
which information you got right.

1242
00:56:22,073 --> 00:56:23,439
It feels like there could be
a foolproof strategy.

1243
00:56:23,440 --> 00:56:25,242
Someone with a lot of money could
test a few combinations first,

1244
00:56:25,243 --> 00:56:27,577
then share the information they get
with just two or three other people.

1245
00:56:27,578 --> 00:56:29,779
- It's possible to figure out the answer.
- Alliances are bound to form.

1246
00:56:29,804 --> 00:56:31,005
And if someone gets it right,

1247
00:56:31,348 --> 00:56:33,316
then the leader could give that person...

1248
00:56:33,317 --> 00:56:35,651
- immunity.
- Then they won't be eliminated that day.

1249
00:56:35,652 --> 00:56:37,986
That makes the leader
incredibly important.

1250
00:56:37,987 --> 00:56:39,088
A leader has a lot of power.

1251
00:56:39,089 --> 00:56:41,290
They can basically guarantee the safety...

1252
00:56:41,291 --> 00:56:42,959
of whoever they want to protect.

1253
00:56:43,594 --> 00:56:45,328
So we both got four points,

1254
00:56:45,329 --> 00:56:48,364
and there's no way to get zero
in any category.

1255
00:56:48,566 --> 00:56:49,766
- One is the minimum.
- Right.

1256
00:56:49,967 --> 00:56:51,868
So if you put one point
in each category either way...

1257
00:56:51,869 --> 00:56:54,637
Our possibilities have already been
narrowed down too much.

1258
00:56:55,439 --> 00:56:59,009
Everyone else's total score is
public too, but...

1259
00:57:01,679 --> 00:57:02,779
The lowest score is four.

1260
00:57:03,247 --> 00:57:04,647
Even if you just guess,
the odds are one in sixteen.

1261
00:57:05,082 --> 00:57:06,315
In my case,

1262
00:57:06,316 --> 00:57:07,817
I'm right, right, left, left.

1263
00:57:07,985 --> 00:57:09,918
Even if someone guessed
one point all the way down the left side,

1264
00:57:09,919 --> 00:57:12,288
they'd still get two
out of the four codes right.

1265
00:57:12,590 --> 00:57:14,057
The other two would be wrong, of course.

1266
00:57:14,391 --> 00:57:15,791
Then on the second try,
they'd get it right.

1267
00:57:17,229 --> 00:57:20,163
In Go Ae Shin's case too,

1268
00:57:20,164 --> 00:57:21,698
two attacks would be enough
to eliminate her.

1269
00:57:21,832 --> 00:57:24,600
Other than that,
Hippo has the highest score.

1270
00:57:24,936 --> 00:57:26,703
Her total is 11 points.

1271
00:57:26,905 --> 00:57:28,358
You could figure it out
by testing a few combinations.

1272
00:57:28,359 --> 00:57:30,540
Two attacks wouldn't necessarily
guarantee her elimination,

1273
00:57:30,541 --> 00:57:31,742
but I think she's in a similar situation.

1274
00:57:31,743 --> 00:57:34,444
So if I don't become the leader,
I'll basically be eliminated.

1275
00:57:34,879 --> 00:57:36,012
The rice is ready.

1276
00:57:37,181 --> 00:57:38,114
Let's set the table, then.

1277
00:57:38,115 --> 00:57:38,915
Let's go!

1278
00:57:43,954 --> 00:57:46,889
Can they really guess
someone's exact scores?

1279
00:57:47,425 --> 00:57:49,159
It doesn't look easy at all, does it?

1280
00:57:49,727 --> 00:57:52,228
But it's not impossible.

1281
00:57:53,364 --> 00:57:54,964
Would you like to form
an alliance with us?

1282
00:57:55,299 --> 00:57:56,532
Is there already an alliance?

1283
00:57:57,101 --> 00:57:58,768
We've been thinking about it.

1284
00:57:58,769 --> 00:58:00,637
We're just asking.

1285
00:58:01,038 --> 00:58:02,405
But you're probably a silver-spoon guy.

1286
00:58:02,940 --> 00:58:05,241
Why do you think that?

1287
00:58:05,242 --> 00:58:06,844
- No?
- I just got that impression.

1288
00:58:06,845 --> 00:58:08,973
There's no clear standard for what counts
as being born rich anyway.

1289
00:58:08,974 --> 00:58:10,146
It's just a feeling.

1290
00:58:10,147 --> 00:58:12,682
You seem like you grew up
pretty sheltered.

1291
00:58:12,683 --> 00:58:14,584
Like you never really struggled.

1292
00:58:14,585 --> 00:58:15,852
I've been through a lot too.

1293
00:58:16,152 --> 00:58:18,321
I'm pretty straightforward.

1294
00:58:18,956 --> 00:58:19,989
Just make sure this is a good one.

1295
00:58:19,990 --> 00:58:21,491
I never betray people.

1296
00:58:21,893 --> 00:58:23,360
- Me neither.
- I know.

1297
00:58:25,696 --> 00:58:27,930
Before we'd even figured out
where everyone stood,

1298
00:58:27,931 --> 00:58:30,333
he suggested to Dark Knight,
"Let's form a group of three."

1299
00:58:30,334 --> 00:58:32,869
I thought that was moving
a little too fast.

1300
00:58:33,571 --> 00:58:35,805
- Then let's shake on it.
- Sure.

1301
00:58:35,806 --> 00:58:36,772
Wait. The mic.

1302
00:58:36,773 --> 00:58:38,040
(Smoking area alliance formed)

1303
00:58:38,041 --> 00:58:40,009
As I mentioned yesterday,

1304
00:58:40,010 --> 00:58:43,079
I voted for both of you
in the favorability vote.

1305
00:58:44,548 --> 00:58:46,048
I voted for you too.

1306
00:58:48,152 --> 00:58:50,019
You didn't? Is it a secret?

1307
00:58:51,021 --> 00:58:51,988
Got it.

1308
00:58:55,092 --> 00:58:56,225
It's still nice to hear that.

1309
00:58:57,828 --> 00:59:00,963
Dark Knight said he voted for me.

1310
00:59:01,365 --> 00:59:04,767
But Gray definitely told me yesterday
that he voted for me too.

1311
00:59:04,768 --> 00:59:06,269
How many votes did you get?

1312
00:59:06,270 --> 00:59:07,197
- In the favorability vote?
- One.

1313
00:59:07,605 --> 00:59:08,771
That's not many.

1314
00:59:09,106 --> 00:59:10,673
That one was from me.

1315
00:59:10,674 --> 00:59:11,608
- Really?
- Yes.

1316
00:59:11,609 --> 00:59:13,109
I only got one vote,

1317
00:59:13,110 --> 00:59:14,577
so one of them has to be lying.

1318
00:59:15,680 --> 00:59:18,915
(Last night)

1319
00:59:20,851 --> 00:59:22,852
(Michael received
one favorability vote on Day One.)

1320
00:59:23,186 --> 00:59:24,821
I thought, "Wait, what?"

1321
00:59:25,422 --> 00:59:28,391
I think that's when the seed
of doubt was planted.

1322
00:59:28,759 --> 00:59:31,627
It's now time to select today's leader.

1323
00:59:32,362 --> 00:59:34,564
The top three residents...

1324
00:59:34,565 --> 00:59:37,500
who received the most votes
in last night's favorability vote...

1325
00:59:37,501 --> 00:59:39,369
will become today's candidates for leader.

1326
00:59:40,070 --> 00:59:41,804
That's why we did the vote!

1327
00:59:42,806 --> 00:59:44,173
Oh, really?

1328
00:59:45,609 --> 00:59:48,377
I was really glad I did that...

1329
00:59:48,378 --> 00:59:49,879
in advance yesterday.

1330
00:59:50,014 --> 00:59:51,614
If I'd known this was coming,

1331
00:59:51,883 --> 00:59:54,617
I would've tried a little harder
to charm everyone yesterday.

1332
00:59:56,086 --> 00:59:57,653
Today's candidates for leader are...

1333
00:59:57,654 --> 01:00:01,092
(Day Two leader candidates)

1334
01:00:01,117 --> 01:00:03,083
Nangja,

1335
01:00:03,995 --> 01:00:06,329
Polar Bear,

1336
01:00:08,532 --> 01:00:11,334
and Sugar.

1337
01:00:11,902 --> 01:00:13,168
Is there anything else we need to do?

1338
01:00:13,193 --> 01:00:14,761
This is the last one.

1339
01:00:15,172 --> 01:00:17,139
We were two of the top three
in yesterday's vote.

1340
01:00:17,140 --> 01:00:18,909
- Really?
- So one of us will become the leader.

1341
01:00:22,179 --> 01:00:23,179
Good for you.

1342
01:00:24,682 --> 01:00:25,715
Congratulations.

1343
01:00:26,183 --> 01:00:29,052
Polar Bear, Nangja, and Sugar.

1344
01:00:29,053 --> 01:00:30,586
All three of them were women.

1345
01:00:30,989 --> 01:00:33,622
"Wait, why are there no men?"

1346
01:00:33,857 --> 01:00:37,260
I thought maybe the women had
mostly voted for other women.

1347
01:00:37,261 --> 01:00:39,461
I was really happy
that all three candidates were women,

1348
01:00:39,462 --> 01:00:42,064
and I was rooting for them.

1349
01:00:42,533 --> 01:00:45,535
(Last night during the favorability vote)

1350
01:00:45,536 --> 01:00:48,304
(Benjamin voted for Go Ae Shin,
Nangja, and Polar Bear.)

1351
01:00:48,305 --> 01:00:50,273
I picked the first three women
on the list.

1352
01:00:50,741 --> 01:00:51,574
I just...

1353
01:00:51,976 --> 01:00:54,077
wanted to see the men and women split up
and go head-to-head for once.

1354
01:00:54,879 --> 01:00:56,379
I wondered if acting a little goofy...

1355
01:00:56,380 --> 01:00:58,514
and taking it easy yesterday...

1356
01:00:58,515 --> 01:00:59,782
had actually worked.

1357
01:00:59,883 --> 01:01:02,150
After spending a long time
in different organizations,

1358
01:01:02,151 --> 01:01:03,420
you start to get a sense...

1359
01:01:03,421 --> 01:01:06,055
of the type to stay under the radar
and last a long time.

1360
01:01:06,056 --> 01:01:08,424
So in that sense, getting a lot
of favorability votes was a good sign.

1361
01:01:08,692 --> 01:01:12,695
(Day Two Prize Money
And Favorability Rankings)

1362
01:01:13,464 --> 01:01:15,498
(If there is a tie
for favorability votes,)

1363
01:01:15,499 --> 01:01:17,867
(the resident with more prize money
becomes a candidate.)

1364
01:01:17,868 --> 01:01:20,403
(Day One leader's term ends.)

1365
01:01:20,404 --> 01:01:22,105
I have to return this too, right?

1366
01:01:22,973 --> 01:01:24,040
That's too bad.

1367
01:01:24,641 --> 01:01:27,043
I've gone from a gold badge
to a silver one.

1368
01:01:31,248 --> 01:01:34,050
Can't we eat this while we listen
to the candidates' campaign promises?

1369
01:01:34,051 --> 01:01:34,917
Sounds great.

1370
01:01:35,719 --> 01:01:37,687
For me,

1371
01:01:38,222 --> 01:01:40,490
the biggest reason I came here was...

1372
01:01:40,491 --> 01:01:43,392
to grow by constantly
hearing different perspectives...

1373
01:01:43,393 --> 01:01:45,595
from all kinds of people.

1374
01:01:45,896 --> 01:01:49,365
I'll do my best to make sure
everyone can...

1375
01:01:49,567 --> 01:01:51,868
share their opinions peacefully.
That's all.

1376
01:01:54,938 --> 01:01:56,171
I love that.

1377
01:01:57,041 --> 01:01:59,609
Ultimately, I think a leader...

1378
01:01:59,610 --> 01:02:01,611
has two main responsibilities.

1379
01:02:01,612 --> 01:02:03,580
First, it's important
to carefully manage...

1380
01:02:03,581 --> 01:02:06,550
the day-to-day affairs
of this community...

1381
01:02:06,551 --> 01:02:08,351
and make sure everything runs smoothly.

1382
01:02:08,485 --> 01:02:11,987
Second, there will be plenty of clashes
between different opinions going forward,

1383
01:02:11,988 --> 01:02:15,791
and a leader needs
to listen to all of them...

1384
01:02:15,792 --> 01:02:18,127
and bring those views together.

1385
01:02:18,128 --> 01:02:19,528
Rather than pushing my own agenda,

1386
01:02:19,529 --> 01:02:22,899
I'll do my best to be
a thoughtful and practical leader...

1387
01:02:22,900 --> 01:02:25,134
who listens carefully to everyone's ideas.

1388
01:02:25,135 --> 01:02:26,235
Thanks for your support.

1389
01:02:27,005 --> 01:02:29,004
The level of campaigning is
totally different from yesterday.

1390
01:02:29,306 --> 01:02:30,539
That was legit.

1391
01:02:31,074 --> 01:02:32,442
I feel like she wouldn't buy us
what we want.

1392
01:02:32,676 --> 01:02:34,677
Maybe she'll be way too frugal.

1393
01:02:34,678 --> 01:02:37,380
No, if you tell me what you want to eat,
I'll write it down.

1394
01:02:37,381 --> 01:02:39,015
"I'll only write it down."

1395
01:02:39,917 --> 01:02:41,217
"I never said I'd buy it for you."

1396
01:02:41,518 --> 01:02:46,188
I'm very confident that I can support...

1397
01:02:46,189 --> 01:02:48,090
all of you well.

1398
01:02:48,091 --> 01:02:50,928
And I think I have a pretty good sense...

1399
01:02:50,929 --> 01:02:54,396
of how to resolve conflicts wisely
when they come up,

1400
01:02:54,397 --> 01:02:56,799
so I believe I can be helpful
in that way too.

1401
01:03:00,604 --> 01:03:02,505
I'll go vote.

1402
01:03:02,506 --> 01:03:03,773
That fast?

1403
01:03:05,810 --> 01:03:08,845
I'm the type who leads
by taking action first.

1404
01:03:08,846 --> 01:03:10,980
What's there to think about
when it comes to voting?

1405
01:03:11,381 --> 01:03:13,415
(The Community: Nangja, Polar Bear, Sugar)

1406
01:03:14,885 --> 01:03:15,918
Benjamin.

1407
01:03:16,353 --> 01:03:17,320
I'll vote for...

1408
01:03:21,925 --> 01:03:23,258
(The Community: Nangja)

1409
01:03:23,259 --> 01:03:25,528
I will now announce the voting results.

1410
01:03:25,729 --> 01:03:28,030
The new leader of the Community House...

1411
01:03:28,031 --> 01:03:29,131
(Day Two Leader)

1412
01:03:29,132 --> 01:03:31,534
is Polar Bear.

1413
01:03:31,969 --> 01:03:33,569
Thank you.

1414
01:03:34,071 --> 01:03:36,906
That's refreshing.

1415
01:03:37,373 --> 01:03:38,774
The leader's badge!

1416
01:03:39,576 --> 01:03:40,643
Bravo!

1417
01:03:41,645 --> 01:03:42,612
Amazing.

1418
01:03:45,115 --> 01:03:47,116
(Polar Bear: six votes)

1419
01:03:47,117 --> 01:03:48,250
(Nangja: four votes)

1420
01:03:48,251 --> 01:03:49,719
(Sugar: two votes)

1421
01:03:50,086 --> 01:03:53,122
Of the three candidates,
I voted for Polar Bear.

1422
01:03:53,123 --> 01:03:56,191
I liked how she could clearly put
her thoughts into words...

1423
01:03:56,192 --> 01:03:59,395
without dragging things out.

1424
01:03:59,396 --> 01:04:00,363
I wanted to see...

1425
01:04:00,364 --> 01:04:02,464
what she'd be like
in an actual leadership position.

1426
01:04:02,633 --> 01:04:04,367
(Polar Bear: 6, Nangja: 4, Sugar: 2)

1427
01:04:04,767 --> 01:04:08,771
Polar Bear has very strong convictions
of her own, but at the same time,

1428
01:04:08,772 --> 01:04:11,674
she's also very open to hearing
and accepting other people's opinions.

1429
01:04:11,675 --> 01:04:12,574
(Gray)

1430
01:04:13,043 --> 01:04:15,878
(Polar Bear: 6, Nangja: 4, Sugar: 2)

1431
01:04:16,054 --> 01:04:18,589
She showed her own style of leadership.

1432
01:04:18,716 --> 01:04:20,082
I'm kind of like that too.

1433
01:04:20,083 --> 01:04:23,385
No matter what your political views are,

1434
01:04:23,386 --> 01:04:25,488
I may disagree with your politics,

1435
01:04:25,689 --> 01:04:28,724
but that doesn't mean
I reject you as a person.

1436
01:04:28,725 --> 01:04:31,460
I like everything about Polar Bear
except that she's a democrat.

1437
01:04:33,830 --> 01:04:36,565
I voted for Nangja.

1438
01:04:36,934 --> 01:04:41,437
I felt that Polar Bear had
very strong opinions of her own.

1439
01:04:41,438 --> 01:04:43,406
(Polar Bear: 6, Nangja: 4, Sugar: 2)

1440
01:04:43,674 --> 01:04:46,309
My choice was Sugar.

1441
01:04:46,744 --> 01:04:49,645
Honestly, I thought the other two would
both do a great job,

1442
01:04:49,646 --> 01:04:52,848
so I was curious to see what it would be
like if Sugar became the leader.

1443
01:04:53,717 --> 01:04:56,051
Polar Bear is very assertive...

1444
01:04:56,052 --> 01:04:58,054
and tends to push her opinions strongly,

1445
01:04:58,288 --> 01:05:02,391
so I thought she might come across
as a little authoritarian.

1446
01:05:02,693 --> 01:05:04,927
From what I saw, it wasn't
about the campaign promises.

1447
01:05:04,928 --> 01:05:08,632
It was more
about the candidates' attitudes.

1448
01:05:08,633 --> 01:05:10,600
I thought people would be
drawn to someone...

1449
01:05:10,801 --> 01:05:13,202
who could confidently lead them...

1450
01:05:13,203 --> 01:05:16,339
through this chaotic situation...

1451
01:05:16,340 --> 01:05:18,274
and wouldn't leave anyone behind,

1452
01:05:18,275 --> 01:05:21,210
so that's what I tried to emphasize.

1453
01:05:21,678 --> 01:05:23,979
I hope Polar Bear becomes the leader.

1454
01:05:26,183 --> 01:05:27,984
If she does,

1455
01:05:28,285 --> 01:05:30,419
we'll learn more about her,

1456
01:05:31,121 --> 01:05:32,354
and then we can take her out.

1457
01:05:33,323 --> 01:05:34,523
(Day Two Leader Vote)

1458
01:05:34,524 --> 01:05:36,592
(Polar Bear: Polar Bear, Ted, Go Ae Shin,
Benjamin, Superman, Gray)

1459
01:05:36,593 --> 01:05:38,360
(Nangja: Hippo, Sugar, Nangja, Genie)

1460
01:05:38,361 --> 01:05:39,995
(Sugar: Dark Knight, Michael)

1461
01:05:46,002 --> 01:05:48,341
(Polar Bear: Day Two Leader)

1462
01:05:48,342 --> 01:05:50,806
(Left, feminism,
working-class, open-minded)

1463
01:05:55,979 --> 01:05:57,213
(Income Earning Activity Rules)

1464
01:05:57,214 --> 01:06:01,083
Starting today, income-earning activities
will begin at the Community House.

1465
01:06:02,085 --> 01:06:03,351
We should start earning money now.

1466
01:06:03,352 --> 01:06:04,553
First,

1467
01:06:04,554 --> 01:06:07,922
income-earning activities are
cooperative programs...

1468
01:06:07,947 --> 01:06:10,080
in which residents can earn
communal prize money...

1469
01:06:10,081 --> 01:06:11,417
based on their performance.

1470
01:06:13,397 --> 01:06:14,397
Second,

1471
01:06:14,665 --> 01:06:18,534
the leader can distribute
the prize money earned...

1472
01:06:18,535 --> 01:06:21,070
according to their own criteria.

1473
01:06:21,071 --> 01:06:22,671
So I'll be distributing the money.

1474
01:06:23,373 --> 01:06:24,373
Third,

1475
01:06:24,508 --> 01:06:27,709
there are six positions available
for each income-earning activity,

1476
01:06:27,979 --> 01:06:30,213
and the leader will decide
who participates.

1477
01:06:30,214 --> 01:06:32,147
Now we need to decide
who's going to go out to work.

1478
01:06:32,148 --> 01:06:33,817
We need six people.

1479
01:06:33,818 --> 01:06:35,554
So you get to decide who's employed
and who's unemployed?

1480
01:06:35,555 --> 01:06:36,452
Fourth,

1481
01:06:36,853 --> 01:06:40,756
the leader may use the communal fund
to create additional positions.

1482
01:06:40,924 --> 01:06:44,993
For an additional position, 1,000 dollars
will be deducted from the communal fund.

1483
01:06:44,994 --> 01:06:46,395
So if we use 1,000 dollars
from the communal fund,

1484
01:06:46,396 --> 01:06:50,399
we'll be able to send seven people out
to earn money for us.

1485
01:06:50,400 --> 01:06:51,700
We can only add one more.

1486
01:06:51,968 --> 01:06:52,935
Fifth,

1487
01:06:53,103 --> 01:06:55,304
residents who do not participate
in the income-earning activity will...

1488
01:06:55,905 --> 01:06:58,441
remain at the house to help...

1489
01:06:58,442 --> 01:07:00,709
with upkeep and spend their time freely.

1490
01:07:00,710 --> 01:07:02,111
So you get to go outside?

1491
01:07:02,446 --> 01:07:05,181
I'm confident in almost
any outdoor activity.

1492
01:07:05,449 --> 01:07:06,315
Me too.

1493
01:07:06,850 --> 01:07:10,319
Now, let me explain
today's income-earning activity.

1494
01:07:10,754 --> 01:07:11,687
That sounds interesting.

1495
01:07:12,490 --> 01:07:15,291
The ability required
for today's activity is...

1496
01:07:15,626 --> 01:07:19,194
a good sense of reality.

1497
01:07:20,831 --> 01:07:23,033
A sense of reality.
That must be a big hint.

1498
01:07:23,034 --> 01:07:25,734
The leader must visit
the Community Center...

1499
01:07:25,735 --> 01:07:29,172
and submit a list of residents...

1500
01:07:29,339 --> 01:07:31,274
except for those they believe
lack a sense of reality.

1501
01:07:31,275 --> 01:07:32,308
Polar Bear!

1502
01:07:32,696 --> 01:07:34,997
This is so hard!
The maximum prize money available...

1503
01:07:35,069 --> 01:07:37,237
through today's income-earning activity...

1504
01:07:37,448 --> 01:07:40,750
is 31,000 dollars.

1505
01:07:40,751 --> 01:07:42,452
- That's the maximum, right?
- Yes.

1506
01:07:42,453 --> 01:07:43,453
That's a lot.

1507
01:07:44,188 --> 01:07:45,922
Anyway, the important thing is...

1508
01:07:45,923 --> 01:07:47,723
that we need to put together...

1509
01:07:47,724 --> 01:07:50,626
an Avengers team that can
actually succeed at the mission.

1510
01:07:50,627 --> 01:07:53,563
This is just my personal opinion,
but when we call someone "realistic,"

1511
01:07:53,864 --> 01:07:55,565
it's often a softer way of saying,

1512
01:07:55,566 --> 01:07:58,468
"You're pretty rational."

1513
01:07:58,469 --> 01:08:00,703
"You don't waste time saying
things that don't matter," right?

1514
01:08:01,037 --> 01:08:04,773
So I think it might not be
a bad idea to focus on people...

1515
01:08:04,774 --> 01:08:06,041
who are more of the Thinking type.

1516
01:08:06,210 --> 01:08:07,777
Personality-wise, right?

1517
01:08:07,778 --> 01:08:09,311
Let's figure out
who's rational and realistic.

1518
01:08:09,579 --> 01:08:10,913
Someone reasonable.

1519
01:08:11,748 --> 01:08:15,751
Then, if we start thinking
about who should come with us,

1520
01:08:15,752 --> 01:08:18,187
first of all, Benjamin and Superman.

1521
01:08:19,022 --> 01:08:20,355
What role should I take, then?

1522
01:08:20,691 --> 01:08:23,192
Both physical and thinking?

1523
01:08:25,395 --> 01:08:26,528
I really wanted to be chosen.

1524
01:08:26,529 --> 01:08:28,230
Let's just work hard at whatever we do.

1525
01:08:28,231 --> 01:08:29,665
I need to show her my loyalty.

1526
01:08:29,666 --> 01:08:33,902
I need to show her just how loyal
a minion I can be to the leader.

1527
01:08:34,271 --> 01:08:35,905
I may be the spy,

1528
01:08:35,906 --> 01:08:38,241
but I still want to show
that I don't want to get eliminated.

1529
01:08:38,742 --> 01:08:40,844
This is a chance to go outside,
so if you want to state your case...

1530
01:08:40,845 --> 01:08:42,511
- please speak up.
- Yes, feel free to tell me.

1531
01:08:42,512 --> 01:08:44,780
Personally, I'd recommend Dark Knight.

1532
01:08:45,849 --> 01:08:48,117
If you think I'd be good...

1533
01:08:48,752 --> 01:08:50,586
- You want to do it?
- I'd recommend him.

1534
01:08:50,988 --> 01:08:52,154
Dark Knight.

1535
01:08:52,865 --> 01:08:55,368
I think Michael has...

1536
01:08:55,393 --> 01:08:57,293
both brains and stamina,

1537
01:08:57,294 --> 01:08:59,162
so I'd recommend him too.

1538
01:08:59,163 --> 01:09:01,898
Personally, I'd recommend Gray.

1539
01:09:01,899 --> 01:09:03,266
He has experience and maturity.

1540
01:09:03,267 --> 01:09:04,935
And a calmness about him.

1541
01:09:04,936 --> 01:09:06,235
But I'm a little worried.

1542
01:09:06,236 --> 01:09:07,938
I'm very responsible,

1543
01:09:07,939 --> 01:09:09,806
so I think I'd take it really hard
if we fail.

1544
01:09:09,807 --> 01:09:10,973
I love that you're responsible.

1545
01:09:13,376 --> 01:09:18,480
(The three smokers alliance recommend
each other.)

1546
01:09:18,481 --> 01:09:21,550
In the end, the final decision is
up to the leader.

1547
01:09:21,551 --> 01:09:23,919
(Selecting participants
for the income-earning activity)

1548
01:09:24,821 --> 01:09:28,257
Then I'll just choose
the last member myself.

1549
01:09:28,258 --> 01:09:29,358
It's a secret, right?

1550
01:09:30,527 --> 01:09:32,628
I gave it a lot of thought.

1551
01:09:33,764 --> 01:09:35,464
First, I chose Superman
because he can handle physical tasks...

1552
01:09:35,465 --> 01:09:38,034
while still staying calm
and making practical decisions.

1553
01:09:38,035 --> 01:09:40,169
Then I chose Benjamin
because he's good with numbers...

1554
01:09:40,170 --> 01:09:42,137
and can make clear-headed judgments...

1555
01:09:42,138 --> 01:09:44,339
based on the information he's given.

1556
01:09:46,843 --> 01:09:48,042
(Income Earning Activity Participants)

1557
01:09:48,043 --> 01:09:51,347
As for Go Ae Shin, I chose her
because she's physically capable as well.

1558
01:09:52,482 --> 01:09:55,485
Personally, I'd like the leader
to come with us,

1559
01:09:55,486 --> 01:09:57,086
give us advice, and help guide the team.

1560
01:09:57,087 --> 01:09:59,788
I'm not very good at this,
but I'll give it a try anyway.

1561
01:09:59,789 --> 01:10:00,656
(Polar Bear)

1562
01:10:04,027 --> 01:10:04,760
(Ted)

1563
01:10:04,761 --> 01:10:07,329
I thought Ted would be good
at quickly anticipating...

1564
01:10:07,330 --> 01:10:10,599
different variables that could come up.

1565
01:10:11,935 --> 01:10:15,471
I really struggled
with choosing the final member.

1566
01:10:17,908 --> 01:10:20,109
I was torn between picking someone...

1567
01:10:20,110 --> 01:10:22,178
with experience on a survival show...

1568
01:10:22,179 --> 01:10:24,647
and choosing someone...

1569
01:10:24,648 --> 01:10:26,383
who seemed to have a wider range...

1570
01:10:26,384 --> 01:10:28,285
of life experience and knowledge.

1571
01:10:28,286 --> 01:10:31,419
But the reason I ruled out the people
with survival show experience was...

1572
01:10:31,420 --> 01:10:34,223
because I thought
one of them could be the spy.

1573
01:10:36,893 --> 01:10:40,195
So in the end, I chose Gray
because he seemed...

1574
01:10:40,464 --> 01:10:42,798
to have more life experience
than the rest of us.

1575
01:10:43,366 --> 01:10:46,435
The residents who will participate
in the activity will now be announced.

1576
01:10:46,436 --> 01:10:47,869
- Don't get hurt, okay?
- You can do it.

1577
01:10:47,870 --> 01:10:49,304
I'm not realistic at all, to be honest.

1578
01:10:49,940 --> 01:10:51,607
- Let's do our best out there.
- Yes.

1579
01:10:51,808 --> 01:10:55,310
Now we just have to go out,
earn the money, and divide it up, right?

1580
01:10:55,645 --> 01:10:58,180
The pressure on the leader keeps
getting heavier by the day.

1581
01:10:58,415 --> 01:10:59,215
Dark Knight.

1582
01:10:59,750 --> 01:11:00,983
- You're not going?
- No.

1583
01:11:01,285 --> 01:11:02,551
I'm not going.

1584
01:11:02,752 --> 01:11:05,854
I tried to put myself out there
and make a stronger case for myself,

1585
01:11:05,855 --> 01:11:07,389
but she still didn't choose me.

1586
01:11:07,591 --> 01:11:09,492
I don't really gel well with Polar Bear.

1587
01:11:10,293 --> 01:11:11,760
I feel like she's going to be
exhausting to deal with.

1588
01:11:12,796 --> 01:11:14,096
Let's have a smoke first.

1589
01:11:14,097 --> 01:11:16,833
(Smoking Area)

1590
01:11:17,534 --> 01:11:19,068
Interesting. What a shame.

1591
01:11:19,069 --> 01:11:21,337
I thought you'd get out there today.

1592
01:11:21,338 --> 01:11:22,371
No, it's okay.

1593
01:11:22,706 --> 01:11:26,341
Let's just talk about something like this.

1594
01:11:26,342 --> 01:11:29,011
Who could be the spy? Something like that.

1595
01:11:29,813 --> 01:11:32,481
Actually, I only got...

1596
01:11:32,482 --> 01:11:34,283
one favorability vote yesterday.

1597
01:11:34,985 --> 01:11:36,118
But Gray told me...

1598
01:11:37,020 --> 01:11:38,420
that he voted for me.

1599
01:11:38,421 --> 01:11:39,888
- I voted for you.
- Right?

1600
01:11:39,889 --> 01:11:43,059
- You can trust me 100 percent.
- I know you did.

1601
01:11:43,060 --> 01:11:44,393
That's why...

1602
01:11:44,627 --> 01:11:46,795
So I think Gray could be the spy.

1603
01:11:47,030 --> 01:11:48,731
Why would he lie if he wasn't?

1604
01:11:48,732 --> 01:11:49,632
Right.

1605
01:11:49,633 --> 01:11:51,834
I even remember exactly
what I said in the interview.

1606
01:11:51,835 --> 01:11:52,601
Right.

1607
01:11:52,970 --> 01:11:54,903
That I did have some concerns about you,

1608
01:11:54,904 --> 01:11:57,973
but you've trusted me...

1609
01:11:57,974 --> 01:11:59,608
and followed my lead so far,

1610
01:11:59,609 --> 01:12:01,309
so I thought, "Okay, let's pick Michael,"
and I chose you.

1611
01:12:01,678 --> 01:12:05,013
I don't suspect you at all.

1612
01:12:05,348 --> 01:12:06,114
Anyway...

1613
01:12:06,115 --> 01:12:08,650
I have a reasonable suspicion about Gray.

1614
01:12:08,885 --> 01:12:10,219
So that's why he looked flustered earlier.

1615
01:12:10,220 --> 01:12:11,887
I'm always loyal.

1616
01:12:12,455 --> 01:12:15,157
Maybe I should play this smarter.

1617
01:12:17,560 --> 01:12:18,393
Gray.

1618
01:12:18,695 --> 01:12:20,229
I tested him a little,

1619
01:12:20,230 --> 01:12:22,097
and he insisted he was 100 percent sure
he voted for me.

1620
01:12:22,699 --> 01:12:24,834
Then I guess you'll have
to decide for yourself.

1621
01:12:26,036 --> 01:12:27,202
It wasn't a lie.

1622
01:12:27,203 --> 01:12:28,137
I know.

1623
01:12:29,472 --> 01:12:31,373
I've heard both sides of the story.

1624
01:12:31,641 --> 01:12:35,345
Honestly, the person I was
most suspicious of was Gray.

1625
01:12:35,346 --> 01:12:37,346
There's a high chance that he lied.

1626
01:12:37,347 --> 01:12:38,880
No matter how I looked at it,

1627
01:12:38,881 --> 01:12:40,416
(Michael: Right, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

1628
01:12:40,417 --> 01:12:42,117
I just didn't think Dark Knight was lying.

1629
01:12:43,687 --> 01:12:45,020
It's going to be fun.

1630
01:12:45,021 --> 01:12:47,122
Because we're all working
toward the same goal together.

1631
01:12:47,257 --> 01:12:50,693
Everyone's packing all kinds
of must-have items for you.

1632
01:12:51,628 --> 01:12:54,697
If you need it, this is a booster.

1633
01:12:54,999 --> 01:12:57,833
- And this is a light.
- A light.

1634
01:12:57,834 --> 01:13:00,936
You can clip it onto your hat or clothes.

1635
01:13:01,071 --> 01:13:02,506
- Thank you.
- No way!

1636
01:13:02,507 --> 01:13:04,740
And just in case,
here's a nail clipper set.

1637
01:13:05,542 --> 01:13:06,542
To cut?

1638
01:13:06,543 --> 01:13:07,776
(Items prepared for the outdoor activity)

1639
01:13:07,777 --> 01:13:09,745
You never know
when simple tools like these...

1640
01:13:10,748 --> 01:13:12,415
might come in handy.

1641
01:13:12,692 --> 01:13:15,928
Dark Knight seems like he'd be an expert
when it comes to survival.

1642
01:13:15,953 --> 01:13:17,119
That must be his specialty.

1643
01:13:17,920 --> 01:13:20,456
It felt really warm, and I was grateful.

1644
01:13:20,457 --> 01:13:22,524
Seeing everyone go out of their way...

1645
01:13:22,525 --> 01:13:25,393
to make sure we had
everything we needed...

1646
01:13:25,394 --> 01:13:27,863
made me think, "This really is a warm,
caring community."

1647
01:13:28,498 --> 01:13:30,266
They were genuinely trying to help us.

1648
01:13:30,267 --> 01:13:34,536
Since we had no idea what we'd need,
they packed up everything they had.

1649
01:13:34,537 --> 01:13:36,839
It kind of felt like having
the whole neighborhood looking out for us.

1650
01:13:37,041 --> 01:13:38,407
I really liked that. It was fun too.

1651
01:13:38,676 --> 01:13:41,477
Honestly, I kept thinking, "Why do
they keep giving us all this stuff?"

1652
01:13:41,478 --> 01:13:43,479
I couldn't exactly refuse it either.

1653
01:13:43,714 --> 01:13:45,681
I appreciated the thought,

1654
01:13:46,249 --> 01:13:47,663
but I also thought
it lacked a sense of reality.

1655
01:13:49,486 --> 01:13:50,686
- Have a safe trip!
- Let's go!

1656
01:13:50,687 --> 01:13:51,587
- See you!
- You got this!

1657
01:13:51,588 --> 01:13:53,622
- You can do it!
- We'll do our best!

1658
01:13:53,723 --> 01:13:56,425
(Income-earning activity team departs.)

1659
01:13:56,426 --> 01:13:58,660
(Community House,
income-earning activity location)

1660
01:14:00,430 --> 01:14:01,397
Let's earn some money.

1661
01:14:01,398 --> 01:14:03,098
Let's get to work!

1662
01:14:04,801 --> 01:14:05,734
What's the activity?

1663
01:14:05,735 --> 01:14:07,736
(Indoor income-earning activity)

1664
01:14:07,737 --> 01:14:08,637
- Goodness.
- What's this?

1665
01:14:09,105 --> 01:14:09,905
What?

1666
01:14:11,007 --> 01:14:12,107
"Fermi Estimation?"

1667
01:14:12,408 --> 01:14:13,509
What?

1668
01:14:13,510 --> 01:14:15,244
- Here's a guide.
- Instructions.

1669
01:14:15,578 --> 01:14:16,878
I think it's like a quiz.

1670
01:14:17,147 --> 01:14:20,582
Welcome to today's
income-earning activity.

1671
01:14:20,917 --> 01:14:24,152
Today's activity is Fermi Estimation.

1672
01:14:24,286 --> 01:14:25,387
I don't know anything about this.

1673
01:14:25,955 --> 01:14:28,758
Fermi Estimation is
the process of estimating...

1674
01:14:28,759 --> 01:14:31,593
an approximate answer to a problem...

1675
01:14:31,594 --> 01:14:34,095
using only basic knowledge
and logical reasoning.

1676
01:14:34,665 --> 01:14:36,665
That means we can all do it
if we use our brains, right?

1677
01:14:36,666 --> 01:14:37,733
No expertise needed.

1678
01:14:37,734 --> 01:14:40,102
- I think it's about logic.
- I think we picked the right members.

1679
01:14:40,803 --> 01:14:42,771
- Good job putting this team together.
- Trust me.

1680
01:14:43,339 --> 01:14:46,809
The activity will proceed as follows.

1681
01:14:47,176 --> 01:14:50,412
Questions will be given
in five levels of difficulty,

1682
01:14:50,413 --> 01:14:52,113
from Level 1 to Level 5.

1683
01:14:52,949 --> 01:14:55,384
The prize for Level 1 is 1,000 dollars,

1684
01:14:55,819 --> 01:14:59,488
and the prize will double with each level.

1685
01:14:59,789 --> 01:15:02,958
Prize money from each
correctly answered level will accumulate,

1686
01:15:03,693 --> 01:15:05,294
and if you complete all five levels,

1687
01:15:05,695 --> 01:15:08,196
you will receive...

1688
01:15:08,197 --> 01:15:09,831
the total accumulated prize money.

1689
01:15:13,703 --> 01:15:17,339
You may request one hint at each level.

1690
01:15:17,574 --> 01:15:20,676
If you use a hint,
the prize money for that level...

1691
01:15:20,677 --> 01:15:22,511
will be reduced by half.

1692
01:15:24,080 --> 01:15:28,550
You will have only one chance
to give an answer at each level,

1693
01:15:29,277 --> 01:15:32,479
and answers within a five percent margin
of error will be accepted.

1694
01:15:33,723 --> 01:15:35,924
Five percent? That's too stingy.

1695
01:15:37,427 --> 01:15:41,296
If you give an incorrect answer,
you may use 500 dollars...

1696
01:15:41,297 --> 01:15:43,432
from the communal fund
to earn another attempt.

1697
01:15:43,900 --> 01:15:46,001
There is no limit to the number
of additional attempts.

1698
01:15:46,704 --> 01:15:48,537
Once a question is presented,

1699
01:15:48,538 --> 01:15:51,640
you may discuss it freely
with no time limit...

1700
01:15:52,175 --> 01:15:54,676
before giving your final answer.

1701
01:15:57,680 --> 01:15:59,581
So there's no time limit.

1702
01:15:59,582 --> 01:16:01,249
It's better since it's a group activity.

1703
01:16:01,517 --> 01:16:02,383
All right.

1704
01:16:03,119 --> 01:16:04,319
- Bring it on.
- Bring it.

1705
01:16:04,921 --> 01:16:09,291
We will now begin
the Fermi Estimation questions.

1706
01:16:09,725 --> 01:16:11,493
Here is the Level One question.

1707
01:16:12,071 --> 01:16:13,672
(Today's income-earning activity:
Fermi Estimation)

1708
01:16:13,697 --> 01:16:18,200
Today is Saturday, October 21, 2023.

1709
01:16:18,468 --> 01:16:21,703
Min Gyu, who lives in Eunpyeong-gu,

1710
01:16:21,704 --> 01:16:25,741
bought three ten-liter food waste bags
at a convenience store.

1711
01:16:26,876 --> 01:16:30,479
He then went to the CGV movie theater
on the second floor...

1712
01:16:30,480 --> 01:16:34,649
of the same building
and paid cash for one ticket...

1713
01:16:34,784 --> 01:16:37,285
for a seat in Row A to an 8 p.m. 2D movie.

1714
01:16:37,421 --> 01:16:40,656
How much did Min Gyu spend in total?

1715
01:16:42,792 --> 01:16:44,166
(Saturday at 8 p.m.:
What is the total price...)

1716
01:16:44,167 --> 01:16:45,961
(of one Row-A movie ticket
and three ten-liter food waste bags?)

1717
01:16:45,962 --> 01:16:49,031
So this is the sense of reality
they were talking about.

1718
01:16:49,732 --> 01:16:51,866
They're testing how in touch...

1719
01:16:51,867 --> 01:16:52,834
with everyday life we are.

1720
01:16:53,469 --> 01:16:55,270
(How much did Min Gyu spend in total
on a movie and the waste bags?)

1721
01:16:55,271 --> 01:16:58,440
"To the CGV movie theater
on the second floor..."

1722
01:16:58,841 --> 01:17:01,142
"A seat in Row A for an 8 p.m. 2D movie."

1723
01:17:01,143 --> 01:17:02,477
So how much is a ticket?

1724
01:17:03,346 --> 01:17:04,780
You get the gist, right?

1725
01:17:05,315 --> 01:17:07,617
Since it's 8 p.m. at CGV,

1726
01:17:07,618 --> 01:17:08,684
it's not an early morning screening.

1727
01:17:09,018 --> 01:17:11,053
It's a regular 2D seat, but Row A is...

1728
01:17:11,054 --> 01:17:12,487
right at the front,

1729
01:17:12,488 --> 01:17:13,422
so it's a little cheaper.

1730
01:17:13,423 --> 01:17:15,324
- The front row is one dollar cheaper.
- Really?

1731
01:17:17,060 --> 01:17:20,195
And it says, "Saturday, October 21, 2023."

1732
01:17:20,763 --> 01:17:23,965
And since it's Saturday,
we have to use the weekend ticket price.

1733
01:17:23,966 --> 01:17:25,800
- That's right.
- So every word...

1734
01:17:25,801 --> 01:17:27,135
Every single word
in the question is a clue.

1735
01:17:27,737 --> 01:17:30,872
- That time is a pretty good time slot.
- Right.

1736
01:17:30,873 --> 01:17:32,642
It's not a discounted time slot.

1737
01:17:32,643 --> 01:17:34,976
It's not a discounted time,
but it is a discounted seat.

1738
01:17:35,211 --> 01:17:36,077
Yes.

1739
01:17:36,779 --> 01:17:39,848
I recently booked a ticket
and went to see a movie by myself.

1740
01:17:39,849 --> 01:17:41,650
It wasn't in Row A, of course,

1741
01:17:41,951 --> 01:17:43,784
but I think I paid
around 17 or 18 dollars.

1742
01:17:43,785 --> 01:17:45,206
On weekends, it's more expensive
than you'd think.

1743
01:17:45,207 --> 01:17:47,822
- Right.
- I think it's about 18 dollars.

1744
01:17:47,823 --> 01:17:48,691
It's really expensive.

1745
01:17:48,692 --> 01:17:51,426
If it's Row A, it should be
a little cheaper than that.

